  "claims": [
    {
      "card_ids": ["C05"],
      "claim": "The closest analogue, a chlorophenyl- and pyridine-containing thiosemicarbazide, was well distributed into rat brain and reported to cross the BBB, directly supporting meaningful CNS access for the query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C08"],
      "claim": "A chlorophenyl/heteroaromatic pyrazoline analogue showed high brain accumulation in mice via active transport, indicating that similar moderately lipophilic heteroaromatic compounds can achieve CNS exposure.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C01"],
      "claim": "Nicotine, a pyridine-containing small molecule, exhibits a very high brain uptake index and rapid brain penetration in rats, supporting that pyridine-containing compounds can readily cross the BBB.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C12"],
      "claim": "A sulfonamide analogue with chlorophenyl and azole groups had a low mouse brain/plasma ratio of 0.07, but its polar/acidic sulfonamide and higher TPSA make this poor outcome less transferable to the query.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C07"],
      "claim": "4-Aminopyridine showed low CSF levels relative to plasma in dogs, but its small polar primary-amine structure differs substantially from the query's lipophilic chlorophenyl/triazoline core, limiting transferability.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "Predicted pass: the closest analogue with chlorophenyl and pyridine moieties crosses the BBB, nicotine evidence supports pyridine-containing CNS penetration, and the query's moderate lipophilicity and low polar surface area favor meaningful access. Poor outcomes from polar sulfonamide and aminopyridine analogues are less transferable.",
